t.card(s('02','EVAL'),'02','Evaluate a small price increase for an invented seller seeking profit. Demand is estimated to be inelastic over the proposed range, other demand conditions unchanged, but costs and the estimate are uncertain.','Inelastic demand can support a revenue gain because the quantity reduction is proportionately smaller. However, uncertain estimates and costs mean a revenue gain need not become a profit gain. Prefer the increase only if the estimate is relevant and the expected price/quantity outcome covers the cost implications; check actual price-times-quantity figures rather than assume success from the coefficient.',['Argument for (2): inelastic response loses proportionately fewer sales (1), can raise total revenue for small change under stated conditions (1). Argument against (2): estimate uncertain (1), costs can offset revenue gain (1). Condition (1): relevant response and favourable net cost outcome. Judgement (2): conditional support (1), verify actual revenue/cost evidence before claiming profit (1). Total 7.'])
t.card(s('02-M01','MECH'),'02-M01','Explain how a small price increase can raise total revenue when demand is locally inelastic and other demand conditions are unchanged.','Price per unit rises while quantity demanded falls by a smaller proportion. The gain per unit can outweigh the lost quantity, so price times quantity and total revenue rise under those conditions.',['Starting change (1): price increase. Because/mechanism (1): inelastic quantity reduction proportionately smaller. Link (1): price gain outweighs lost quantity for the small change. Consequence (1): total revenue rises with other demand conditions unchanged. Total 4.'])
t.card(s('02-M01','APP'),'02-M01','Explain the revenue implication in an invented case with unchanged other demand conditions: price rises from N$10 to N$11 and sales fall from 100 to 95, giving inelastic demand.','The relatively small sales reduction is outweighed by the higher price per unit; revenue rises from N$1,000 to N$1,045, though profit still depends on costs.',['Context (1): inelastic response in supplied figures. Reason/consequence (1): price gain outweighs sales loss, raising revenue to N$1,045; no automatic profit claim.'])
t.card(s('02-M02','MECH'),'02-M02','Explain how spare capacity and accessible materials can make a producer’s supply more responsive to a higher price in the short run.','The higher price creates an incentive to offer more. Unused equipment time and available materials permit extra output without first building new capacity, so quantity supplied can respond more readily in the period.',['Starting change (1): higher price incentive. Because/mechanism (1): spare equipment capacity. Link (1): accessible materials permit extra production. Consequence (1): stronger quantity-supplied response within the period. Total 4.'])
t.card(s('02-M02','APP'),'02-M02','Explain why supply may be less price responsive for a producer already at full capacity than for one with usable spare equipment and materials.','The constrained producer cannot readily expand output in the period, while the other can use available capacity, so their quantity-supplied responses can differ.',['Context (1): full versus spare capacity. Reason/conclusion (1): differing feasibility of extra output changes responsiveness.'])
t.card(s('03','FEATURE'),'03','Identify the two percentage changes needed to calculate price elasticity of demand.','Percentage change in quantity demanded and percentage change in price.',['Feature (1): quantity demanded percentage. Distinction (1): own-price percentage.'])
t.card(s('03-M01','CALC'),'03-M01','Calculate PED magnitude using original bases for an invented market where price rises from N$10 to N$11 and weekly quantity demanded falls from 100 to 95, other demand conditions unchanged.','Quantity proportion = −5/100 = −0.05, equivalent to −5%. Price proportion = 1/10 = 0.1, equivalent to 10%. Using the percentage values, PED = −5/10 = −0.5, magnitude 0.5, inelastic.',['Formula and substitution/working (1): −5% ÷ 10%. Units/interpretation (1): magnitude 0.5, a dimensionless inelastic-demand coefficient. Total 2.'])
t.card(s('03-M01','INTERP'),'03-M01','Analyse the meaning of a PED magnitude of 0.5 in an invented market where a 10% price rise is associated with a 5% fall in quantity demanded, other demand conditions unchanged.','The quantity-demanded response is proportionately smaller than the price change, so demand is inelastic. The 5% reduction is still a response, so it is not perfectly inelastic. The estimate describes the stated comparison, not every possible future price.',['Relationship/pattern (1): proportionately smaller quantity response/inelastic. Evidence/comparison (1): 5% versus 10%. Explanation/reason (2): non-zero response distinguishes perfect inelasticity (1), scope restricted to stated comparison (1). Total 4.'])
t.card(s('03-M02','CALC'),'03-M02','Calculate PES using original bases when an invented producer’s price rises from N$20 to N$22 and weekly quantity supplied rises from 50 to 60, other supply conditions unchanged.','Quantity-supplied proportion = 10/50 = 0.2, equivalent to 20%. Price proportion = 2/20 = 0.1, equivalent to 10%. Using the percentage values, PES = 20/10 = 2, elastic.',['Formula and substitution/working (1): 20% ÷ 10%. Units/interpretation (1): coefficient 2, dimensionless and elastic supply. Total 2.'])
t.card(s('03-M02','INTERP'),'03-M02','Analyse the meaning of PES 2 when an invented producer supplies 20% more after a 10% own-price rise, other conditions unchanged. Spare capacity is available.','Supply responds proportionately more than price, so it is elastic in the stated period. The 20% versus 10% evidence is consistent with using spare capacity to expand output; it does not establish that every later price rise produces the same coefficient.',['Relationship/pattern (1): elastic supply/proportionately larger Qs response. Evidence/comparison (1): 20% versus 10%. Explanation/reason (2): available capacity permits output response (1), scope limited to stated conditions/period (1). Total 4.'])
