t.card(p,'01 03 02','Invented teaching data, using original bases: a notebook price rises from N$10 to N$11 and weekly quantity demanded changes from 100 to 95, with other demand conditions unchanged. A producer’s price rises from N$20 to N$22 and quantity supplied rises from 50 to 60, with other supply conditions unchanged and spare capacity available. Describe three features of the elasticity calculations (6). Explain the demand and supply coefficients and their economic meaning (7). Discuss a proposed notebook price increase for profit, considering the demand response, costs and the reliability of these estimates (7).','Elasticity uses percentage rather than raw changes, divides quantity response by price change and distinguishes Qd from Qs. The demand calculation gives −5%/10%=−0.5, magnitude0.5 and inelastic; supply gives20%/10%=2 and elastic. Demand changes proportionately less, while spare capacity can support the larger supply response. Notebook revenue changes from N$1,000 to N$1,045 in the supplied case. That supports a revenue argument, but costs and relevance of estimates still determine profit. Prefer the proposed increase only if a credible response and cost assessment support it.',['Context/data description (6): percentage changes (1), original bases stated (1); Q response in numerator (1), price response denominator (1); demand uses Qd (1), supply uses Qs (1). Mechanism/explanation (7): demand−5% (1), price+10% (1), PED magnitude0.5/meaning inelastic (1); supply+20% (1), price+10% (1), PES2/meaning elastic (1); capacity can support supply response (1). Both sides/judgement (7): inelastic demand supports revenue argument (1), actual receipts N$1,045 versus N$1,000 (1); costs may offset gain (1), estimate may not remain relevant (1); condition credible response and cost outcome (1); conditional recommendation (1), no revenue-to-profit guarantee (1). Marks allocation 6+7+7=20.'])
