t.card(p,'02','Evaluate a small price increase in two seven-mark plans for an invented seller seeking revenue while monitoring costs. Case 1: demand magnitude is estimated at 0.5 over the proposed range. Case 2: demand magnitude is estimated at 2 over that range. Other demand conditions are unchanged in each case, but estimates have uncertainty and output must be feasible.','Case 1: inelastic demand means a proportionately smaller quantity loss, supporting a revenue gain for the small change. Costs and estimate uncertainty remain disadvantages; conditional support requires a relevant estimate and acceptable costs. Case 2: elastic demand means a relatively large quantity loss, which tends to reduce revenue after the price rise. A higher price per unit is a possible benefit but may be outweighed. Prefer avoiding the increase for a revenue objective unless stronger evidence changes the expected response; calculate actual endpoints and consider costs before claiming profit.',['Case 1 marks (7): argument for higher price per unit (1), smaller proportional sales loss supports revenue (1); argument against estimate uncertainty (1), cost effects (1); condition relevant locally inelastic response (1); judgement conditionally supports increase (1), distinguishes revenue from profit (1). Case 2 allocation (7): argument for higher price per unit (1), may reduce pressure to supply units (1); argument against larger proportional quantity loss (1), likely revenue reduction under elastic demand (1); condition relevance of response estimate (1); judgement avoids revenue-seeking increase (1), subject to better evidence/actual endpoint check (1). Total 14.'])
